#: ../../source/docs/arming-your-plane.rst:5
#: 7449bb23c8cd4b26a050d0e912d2d7ca
msgid "Arming Plane"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/arming-your-plane.rst:7
#: 661e5e0a245347f59551ae05e28d76a1
msgid "Before you can fly your plane you need to arm it. Arming the aircraft before flight has two purposes:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/arming-your-plane.rst:10
#: 9219293bc6464cf9a24422b7d96c8688
msgid "prevent the motor from turning when the pilot is not ready to fly (a safety feature)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/arming-your-plane.rst:12
#: 8f6170799e26445b85187f47764bf9c1
msgid "prevent takeoff before the autopilot is fully configured and ready to fly"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/arming-your-plane.rst:15
#: 03a3445e25574c9da0e3c81c1273ad02
msgid "The key thing that arming does is to enable the motor. You will not be able to start the motor (ie. control the throttle) until the aircraft is armed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/arming-your-plane.rst:19
#: b5155ef24c6d4d03bfceedd5d6653983
msgid "**Note**: If you have :ref:`AHRS_EKF_TYPE<AHRS_EKF_TYPE>` set to 2 or 3 (you are using one of the EKFs) then it is particularly important that you have arming checks enabled for INS. Flying EKF without arming checks may cause a crash."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/arming-your-plane.rst:24
#: b73ce97df2ec4c63af65fb074f3d0d1b
msgid "This feature in no way removes the need to respect the prop! When the plane is powered, ALWAYS avoid placing hands in the vicinity of the propeller, even when the throttle is disarmed. If all is not well with the autopilot electronics or software there is always a slight possibility that signal could unintentionally reach the motor. Even though this is unlikely (and made even less likely by safety features such as this) it only takes one time to chew up a finger or hand!"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/arming-your-plane.rst:34
#: 162efec4cc2946ce9d8aab275c004b6d
msgid "Configuring Arming"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/arming-your-plane.rst:36
#: d6cde0642a1947b8a8724bf13146167b
msgid "There are three parameters which control how arming works:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/arming-your-plane.rst:38
#: d0eec628f6ba4976b9e8c805f5c304dd
msgid ":ref:`ARMING_REQUIRE<ARMING_REQUIRE>`: this controls whether an arming step is required. The default is 1, meaning that arming is required before takeoff. If set to 0 then arming is not required (the plane starts off armed)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/arming-your-plane.rst:42
#: b1b614a9c2174e98ac18b32c94be542e
msgid ":ref:`ARMING_SKIPCHK<ARMING_SKIPCHK>`: this controls what checks the autopilot does before arming is allowed. The default is 0, meaning all checks are done. Most users should leave it at 0, as the arming checks are important to ensure the autopilot is ready. See below."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/arming-your-plane.rst:46
#: ddd17ef4065f432887c9055dba23f219
msgid ":ref:`ARMING_RUDDER<ARMING_RUDDER>`: This parameter allows you to configure rudder based arming/disarming. The default is 1, meaning you are able to arm with right rudder. If you set this to 2 you can also disarm with left rudder. If you set this to 0 then you will only be able to arm/disarm via a ground station or RC channel input using its RCx_OPTION."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/arming-your-plane.rst:52
#: 39aba51396d24d19b0ca6657f48149d9
msgid "if :ref:`ARMING_RUDDER<ARMING_RUDDER>` is set to 2, then held left rudder can disarm the vehicle in ANY mode, if the autopilot judges that the vehicle is not flying. This flying check CAN be incorrect under certain extreme circumstances, and disarming of the vehicle while still in flight, while rare, could occur."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/arming-your-plane.rst:55
#: b62ad36a8ec74beeb6749e9b2f9e5c5f
msgid "Arming Checks"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/arming-your-plane.rst:57
#: 5f230c1f0cdb4ecc8069e105507da272
msgid "Before allowing arming the autopilot checks a set of conditions. All conditions must pass for arming to be allowed. If any condition fails then a message explaining what failed is set to the GCS. Any or all of the Pre-Arming Checks can be disabled, but it is not recommended. See the :ref:`common-prearm-safety-checks` topic for more information."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/arming-your-plane.rst:63
#: d9612710a9fe4f519d55147e4ae2d210
msgid "IMPORTANT: RC Transmitter Calibration"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/arming-your-plane.rst:65
#: 43eac8db293d4fdf889101311851813d
msgid "It is essential that your RC radio transmitter be calibrated correctly before continuing. Please see the :ref:`Calibrate your RC input <common-radio-control-calibration>` wiki page if you don't know how to calibrate your radio."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/arming-your-plane.rst:68
#: 381a64e2cb524097a0c3ad42db178251
msgid "When calibrating your RC input you should also be careful to set the minimum value of the throttle (usually RC3_MIN) to the minimum value when in normal flight control. Don't set it to the value used by your transmitter when in throttle failsafe or you won't be able to arm using the rudder as the autopilot will think you are at a non-zero throttle level."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/arming-your-plane.rst:75
#: b337e6c26a414d20b714fb4936daf5e6
msgid "How to Arm"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/arming-your-plane.rst:77
#: f3b8302e987c4d189de721493f88ca8a
msgid "When you are ready to fly you can ask Plane to arm. This can be done in three ways:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/arming-your-plane.rst:80
#: 4d1f7cd7d3f54abfa1baf6a96e2a8606
msgid "**Rudder Arming**. Hold the rudder stick fully to the right and the throttle stick fully down for 3 seconds."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/arming-your-plane.rst:83
#: 18fcc0cef63d4fe4a6d6364c31b29bc7
msgid "when rudder arming in QuadPlanes with an autotakeoff, the motors will spin at :ref:`Q_M_SPIN_ARM<Q_M_SPIN_ARM>` and not takeoff until the rudder stick is returned to neutral. Similarly, for normal plane MODE TAKEOFF, or NAV_TAKEOFFs, the arming will not actually occur until the rudder stick is returned to neutral to prevent the takeoff starting with full right rudder."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/arming-your-plane.rst:85
#: bacc2868055848bc8fae9b45ac1cb8de
msgid "If setup, you can use one of the **RC_xOPTION switches** that includes that function. See switch option \"153\", \"154, or \"160\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/arming-your-plane.rst:86
#: 8ab445abe6cf4f6ab9750905a9911ad0
msgid "**GCS Arming**. Press the arming button on your ground station"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/arming-your-plane.rst:91
#: cd6eb8bf416f42dc996fa2b463a07557
msgid "Location of the Arm/Disarm button in Mission Planner (button circled in red near the bottom of the image)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/arming-your-plane.rst:93
#: 28dbb668c43849d58492b4f5f3e988a9
msgid "Plane does not allow arming in RTL, QRTL, or QLAND modes. Arming in other throttle controlled modes, like LOITER,CRUISE,etc. is possible, but only if actually flying (ie after an accidental inflight disarm, watchdog reset, etc.)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/arming-your-plane.rst:96
#: 82f96280ebf74cd8918f00405c4eb42f
msgid "How to Disarm"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/arming-your-plane.rst:98
#: 8ec35795f0d6434c8489b9f2906fec1f
msgid "If setup, you can use one of the ``RC_xOPTION`` switches that includes that function. See switch option \"81\", \"153, or \"154\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/arming-your-plane.rst:100
#: 2b705daa92f5499a847646f134477cf3
msgid "This is **UNCONDITIONAL**. If done while in flight, all motors disarm and you must have throttle at idle before re-arming can occur!"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/arming-your-plane.rst:102
#: bbf61db983534386b57a578e339beb0f
msgid "It is also possible to disarm using the transmitter. This is done holding throttle at minimum and rudder to the left for 2 seconds. In ArduPlane this condition could be accidentally triggered by pilots while flying so there are additional requirements prior to disarm:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/arming-your-plane.rst:107
#: 4c9f76f47e8448ee969d3343068ee88a
msgid "You need to allow rudder disarming by changing **ARMING_RUDDER** parameter to 2 (ArmOrDisarm) or use the ARM/DISARM switch function provided by setting an RC channel's RCx_OPTION to 153."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/arming-your-plane.rst:110
#: 3eea73934782437b81cc8f5db28af6fd
msgid "The autopilot needs to make sure that you are not actually flying. There is an algorithm for this that uses the **airspeed sensor** readings. So you need this source available and giving values lower enough (in a windy day you might not be able to disarm even landed if the plane thinks you are still flying)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/arming-your-plane.rst:116
#: 78996615eb8349ff8323d2bdac92cee6
msgid "You can also disarm without using the transmitter with one of the following methods:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/arming-your-plane.rst:119
#: 5be960517d364831b29dee6b9c57a5e6
msgid "use a ground station to issue a disarm command"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/arming-your-plane.rst:120
#: e667d6dc5ba243e7bcab07ed56ea6b10
msgid "use the safety switch on your aircraft (if using a :ref:`common-safety-switch-pixhawk`)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/arming-your-plane.rst:121
#: 2b69655062e3428e9e94674fddd696ca
msgid "after an auto-landing (either via mission or QLAND in QuadPlane) the plane will automatically disarm after 20 seconds if still on the ground (controlled by :ref:`LAND_DISARMDELAY<LAND_DISARMDELAY>` parameter)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/arming-your-plane.rst:126
#: 4a96dc87e98a48b698ed9a4679aa4403
msgid "Visual and Audible signals"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/arming-your-plane.rst:128
#: cb8341ef6a234d8e8270347fd89b6a20
msgid "ArduPlane will provide visual and audio clues to the arming state if your autopilot has notification LEDs and a buzzer. The clues are:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/arming-your-plane.rst:131
#: f6154d24eef44eba8e2995ee253171aa
msgid "if the autopilot is disarmed, but is ready to arm then the large 3-colour LED will be flashing green"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/arming-your-plane.rst:133
#: 1d9ff5339bb34c7bbac464fd2fce17d4
msgid "if the autopilot is armed and ready to fly the large 3-colour LED is solid green"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/arming-your-plane.rst:135
#: 5eb657db524240968f42ca8186659ecf
msgid "when the autopilot is ready to arm it will play a \"ready to arm\" sound on the buzzer"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/arming-your-plane.rst:137
#: 7d7c2301a31e486c9cb0226efa39e359
msgid "when the autopilot is armed or disarmed it will play the corresponding sound"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/arming-your-plane.rst:140
#: f2e0aa9b9fa6466fb7f1eef5decc13bf
msgid "See the :ref:`sounds page <common-sounds-pixhawkpx4>` to listen to what the buzzer sounds like for each state."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/arming-your-plane.rst:143
#: 23506b9a12d44257b0cf9c60a21645b7
msgid "Unless the :ref:`ARMING_OPTIONS<ARMING_OPTIONS>` bit 1 is set, text messages will be sent to the GCS to indicate when arming or disarming has occurred."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/arming-your-plane.rst:146
#: 62f945514ae544769940a17243734dd3
msgid "Throttle output when disarmed"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/arming-your-plane.rst:148
#: 9bb12d022ce74317b449aac29e3195ba
msgid "When the plane is disarmed the throttle channel will not respond to pilot input. There are two possible behaviors you can configure:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/arming-your-plane.rst:151
#: 40adc10602be47c4ae3202dc7f4858e7
msgid ":ref:`ARMING_REQUIRE<ARMING_REQUIRE>` = 1. When disarmed the minimum value for the throttle channel (normally RC3_MIN) will be sent to the throttle channel"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/arming-your-plane.rst:153
#: 930598157f4b4ee1b4599e85d798129a
msgid ":ref:`ARMING_REQUIRE<ARMING_REQUIRE>` = 2. When disarmed no pulses are sent to the throttle channel. Note that some ESCs will beep to complain that they are powered on without a control signal or even refuse to initialize and operate."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/arming-your-plane.rst:158
#: abf8a37183804eb69141afecd66c8968
msgid "Diagnosing failure to arm"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/arming-your-plane.rst:160
#: b3e2aff7839540849784b036f8a1309e
msgid "It can be frustrating if your plane refuses to arm. To diagnose arming issues follow this guide"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/arming-your-plane.rst:164
#: e3a0c59187cb4415a4ef6fb68b200380
msgid "In Landing Sequence Pre-Arm Failure"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/arming-your-plane.rst:166
#: 21d8400731a34ed6847fbe2cbbca0a50
msgid "If the last mission item was a landing sequence or land command, then arming is prevented and a \"Prearm: In Landing Sequence\" message is presented. This can occur after an autoland, or if the autopilot is booted before RC is established and the failsafe mode uses a landing sequence (ie :ref:`common-do-land-start` and :ref:`RTL_AUTOLAND<RTL_AUTOLAND>` is set, QLAND, etc.)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/arming-your-plane.rst:168
#: fe5f771482bb48c688d5f6329125ae23
msgid "This may be cleared by restarting the mission using the GCS or :ref:`common-auxiliary-functions` switch, or rebooting with RC active."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/arming-your-plane.rst:171
#: 5cd3f30a861f4f67acadefdb2ddd3a26
msgid "Check that it is ready to arm"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/arming-your-plane.rst:173
#: 7e96444067e845bab61fd1bc72286443
msgid "If your board has a \"ready to arm\" LED (the large LED in the middle of the board on a Pixhawk, for example) then that LED should be flashing green when the board is ready to arm. If it is flashing yellow then that indicates that one of the arming checks is not passing."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/arming-your-plane.rst:179
#: db5688898bc4451aacb84aded42849e5
msgid "Try arming"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/arming-your-plane.rst:181
#: 9217eddbe4b747f0adf2b5b8f1ce2463
msgid "Try sending an arm command with your GCS. If arming is refused then a message will be sent from the autopilot to the GCS indicating why it is refusing to arm."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/arming-your-plane.rst:186
#: ca7772c8ffbc468db86ae18da176cded
msgid "Rudder arming"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/arming-your-plane.rst:188
#: 3b910e05e0ab490e9ce14de3e7b33d92
msgid "If you are using right-rudder + zero-throttle to arm and you don't get a message on your GCS giving a arming failure reason then it may be that your RC calibration is a bit off and the autopilot is not quite seeing zero throttle or isn't quite seeing full right rudder."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/arming-your-plane.rst:194
#: 9a3df40fadbd41dfb9ad96cc000cfc83
msgid "Reasons for refusing to arm"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/arming-your-plane.rst:196
#: 0993dc3a069849cb88f98386faaea11d
msgid "When the autopilot refuses to arm it sends a STATUSTEXT MAVLink message to the GCS explaining why it is refusing. Some possible reasons why the autopilot can refuse to arm are (See the :ref:`common-prearm-safety-checks` topic for more information):"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/arming-your-plane.rst:200
#: cec4cb7e210144ba8537bf7880e8acf3
msgid "**barometer not healthy**. This is very rare. If it happens repeatedly then you may have a barometer hardware fault."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/arming-your-plane.rst:202
#: e267e681beea471ea54895ae96330f99
msgid "**airspeed not healthy**. If you have a airspeed sensor fitted and the autopilot is not getting an airspeed reading it will refuse to arm."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/arming-your-plane.rst:205
#: cb287758d0304dcc9f74781104b0974d
msgid "**logging not available**. If your microSD card has failed or is corrupt then logging won't be available and you cannot arm."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/arming-your-plane.rst:207
#: 01a3f6cb201a4eeda9e29888dde1f8f0
msgid "**gyros not healthy**. If the gyros have failed the autopilot will refuse to arm. This is rare, and if it happens repeatedly then you may have a hardware failure."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/arming-your-plane.rst:210
#: b70da78892a34e73a54b23c7743c671d
msgid "**gyros not calibrated**. This happens when the automatic gyro calibration at startup didn't converge. Try rebooting the autopilot with the plane held still."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/arming-your-plane.rst:213
#: 1257a941b62742ad884584e8fd8db476
msgid "**accels not healthy**. If the accelerometers have failed the autopilot will refuse to arm. Try recalibrating your accelerometers."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/arming-your-plane.rst:215
#: e0ce592eb7d54efd99d898f9c6f18f36
msgid "**GPS accuracy errors**. There are 4 types of GPS arming errors that can be reported. They are \"GPS vert vel error\", \"GPS speed error\", \"GPS horiz error\", \"GPS numsats\". Try moving your plane for better GPS reception or switching off any RF sources (such as a FPV transmitter) that may be interfering with your GPS."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/arming-your-plane.rst:220
#: a9bfb41819ed47af82f3db7db2756e02
msgid "**Mag yaw error**. This happens when your compass is badly out of alignment. Check your compass orientation and re-do your compass calibration or move your plane further away from any magnetic materials."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/arming-your-plane.rst:224
#: 8094b7cd70d641c0b751952a4f0b19ee
msgid "**EKF warmup**. This happens when the EKF is still warming up. Wait another 10 seconds and try again."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/arming-your-plane.rst:226
#: 85f69231a8954813b7369ea74866b028
msgid "**AHRS not healthy**. This means the EKF is not healthy. Often this is due to large variations in GPS position and/or velocity reports, even if a solid 3D lock is reported by the GPS and HDOP is low. Be sure your GPS has a clear \"view\" of the sky with no obstructions. If the error persists then try rebooting your board."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/arming-your-plane.rst:227
#: 9e6d4e453d73449d8eeb143194ec2174
msgid "**3D accel cal needed**. This happens when you have not done a 3D accelerometer calibration."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/arming-your-plane.rst:229
#: 1284eb6dc7b349bc9c74cc807846eef6
msgid "**Inconsistent accelerometers**. This happens when you have multiple IMUs (such as the Pixhawk which has two) and they are not consistent. This can be caused by temperature changes. If the error doesn't clear itself after a minute you will need to redo your accelerometer calibration."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/arming-your-plane.rst:234
#: 51657ce9c7cb4aa9b94b0d56a3217bf3
msgid "**Inconsistent gyros**. This happens when you have multiple gyros and they are not reporting consistent values. If the error does not clear itself after 30 seconds then you will need to reboot."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/arming-your-plane.rst:237
#: effe85779cb74aac997b86ff66304976
msgid "**Limit errors**. The arming checks some of your parameter settings to make sure they are in a reasonable range. The checks are \"ROLL_LIMIT_DEG too small\", \"PTCH_LIM_MAX_DEG too small\", \"PTCH_LIM_MIN_DEG too large\", \"invalid THR_FS_VALUE\"."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/arming-your-plane.rst:241
#: 82f2771e92354763a7b708946e4d9a66
msgid "**GPS n has not been fully configured**. This happens with a uBlox GPS where the GPS driver is unable to fully configure the GPS for the settings that are being requested. This can be caused by a bad wire between the autopilot and GPS, or by a bad response from the GPS. If the message is about \"GPS 0\" then it is the first GPS. If it is \"GPS 1\" then it is the 2nd GPS. If you get a failure for the 2nd GPS and don't have two GPS modules installed then set GPS_TYPE2 to zero to disable the 2nd GPS"
msgstr ""
